From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HeDo <ldileo@digipa.it>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:55:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] toOcta(Point3f) returns (0, 0) for a vector of length
 zero

The octahedron encoding of a float vector divided by |x| + |y| + |z|. A zero vector gave
0 / 0 = NaN, and converting that NaN to int is undefined (UBSan: "-nan is outside the range of
representable values of type 'int'"; x86 gives INT_MIN). The normal predictor of the encoder
calls it with the sum of the face normals around a vertex, which is zero for a vertex of only
degenerate (collinear or coincident) triangles; the INT_MIN then overflowed the deltas and the
bit widths computed from them.

The Point3i overload already returns (0, 0) for a zero vector; this does the same. A vector of
non-zero length is encoded as before.
---
 include/corto/normal_attribute.h | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/include/corto/normal_attribute.h b/include/corto/normal_attribute.h
index 939e749..eb19429 100644
--- a/include/corto/normal_attribute.h
+++ b/include/corto/normal_attribute.h
@@ -73,8 +73,12 @@ public:
 	//Conversion to Octahedron encoding.
 
 	static Point2i toOcta(Point3f v, int unit) {
+		auto len = fabs(v[0]) + fabs(v[1]) + fabs(v[2]);
+		if(len == 0)
+			return Point2i(0, 0);
+
 		Point2f p(v[0], v[1]);
-		p /= (fabs(v[0]) + fabs(v[1]) + fabs(v[2]));
+		p /= len;
 
 		if(v[2] < 0) {
 			p = Point2f(1.0f - fabs(p[1]), 1.0f - fabs(p[0]));
